&lt;p class=&#34;wp-block-paragraph&#34;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thanksgiving&lt;/strong&gt; shopping is no longer just about who offers the deepest discounts. Increasingly, it is about who makes buying something easiest when consumers are away from their computers. That shift could create an opportunity for &lt;strong&gt;Apple Inc.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a data-ticker=&#34;AAPL&#34; data-exchange=&#34;NASDAQ&#34; href=&#34;https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AAPL&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; class=&#34;ticker-link&#34;&gt;(NASDAQ:AAPL)&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Amazon.com, Inc.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a data-ticker=&#34;AMZN&#34; data-exchange=&#34;NASDAQ&#34; href=&#34;https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMZN&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; class=&#34;ticker-link&#34;&gt;(NASDAQ:AMZN)&lt;/a&gt; and digital payment companies as &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.benzinga.com/topic/e-commerce&#34;&gt;smartphone-driven purchases&lt;/a&gt; become an increasingly important part of America&amp;#8217;s biggest shopping season.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class=&#34;wp-block-heading&#34;&gt;Smartphones Are Becoming Shopping Malls&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class=&#34;wp-block-paragraph&#34;&gt;Adobe &lt;a href=&#34;https://business.adobe.com/resources/holiday-shopping-report.html&#34;&gt;expects&lt;/a&gt; Americans to spend $4.4 billion shopping on mobile devices on Thanksgiving Day 2026, an 11% increase from 2025. Even more telling, mobile devices are forecast to account for 63% of Thanksgiving&amp;#8217;s online sales, up from 61.6% last year, according to Adobe&amp;#8217;s holiday shopping forecast.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class=&#34;wp-block-paragraph&#34;&gt;The broader opportunity is significant. Adobe expects total Thanksgiving online spending to reach $6.9 billion, up 8.5% year over year. That means mobile shopping is no longer a secondary channel retailers can afford to overlook. It is becoming central to how consumers discover deals, compare prices and complete purchases.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class=&#34;wp-block-paragraph&#34;&gt;For investors, the shift creates several potential beneficiaries across the shopping ecosystem, although not all of them capture spending in the same way.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class=&#34;wp-block-heading&#34;&gt;Apple Owns the Device. Amazon Owns the Destination.&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class=&#34;wp-block-paragraph&#34;&gt;For Apple, the opportunity begins with the device itself. The &lt;strong&gt;iPhone&lt;/strong&gt; is increasingly the shopping screen consumers carry everywhere, while &lt;strong&gt;Apple Pay&lt;/strong&gt; makes completing purchases easier. Shoppers can use Face ID or Touch ID to authenticate payments in supported apps and websites without repeatedly entering card and shipping details.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class=&#34;wp-block-paragraph&#34;&gt;However, investors should distinguish between a growing mobile shopping market and direct revenue gains for Apple. The company does not earn a percentage of every retail purchase simply because an iPhone user shops on the device. Its opportunity is broader: strengthening the value of its hardware and services ecosystem.&lt;/p&gt;



